\section{What may be banded}{

The banded variable must be \strong{increasing in \code{.t} at unit slope}, which
permits exactly three shapes:
\itemize{
\item a time-invariant variable, e.g. \code{.i$pension} -- this \emph{gates} exposure,
since the individual is in one band throughout;
\item \code{.t} itself -- this \emph{clips} exposure to calendar bounds;
\item \code{.t} minus a time-invariant \code{datey} expression, e.g. \code{.t - .i$retirement},
\code{.t - min(.i$entry, .i$retirement)} (and \code{.x}, which is \code{.t - .b}) -- this
clips exposure to bounds measured from that origin.
}

A decreasing expression such as \code{2020 - .t} is refused: it would flip the
band to \verb{(a, b]}, so adjacent bands would either double-count or drop an
instant.
}

\section{Band edges}{

The edges are \code{c(from, thresholds, to)}, and a \code{NULL} bound means
unbounded. \code{by} generates the interior thresholds from \code{from} and \code{to}, and
must divide \code{to - from} exactly. \code{by} and \code{thresholds} cannot both be given.

So \code{ages(65, 95, by = 5)} gives six bands and excludes the outside, because
both bounds were supplied, while
\code{bands(.i$pension, thresholds = c(5000, 10000, 20000))} gives four bands
open at both ends, because neither was.
}

\section{Missing values}{

A \code{NaN} banded variable compares \code{FALSE} against every threshold, following
IEEE 754, so such a record falls in no band and is simply absent. Note that
base R differs here: \code{NaN < 5} is \code{NA} in R, which needs a third logical
state \strong{logmu} does not have.
}

\section{Combining}{

Includes (and indicators) combine with \code{&}, which intersects: the result
selects the time within \emph{both} operands. Internally an include is a
conjunction of terms -- interval bounds plus indicator gates -- resolved
together by \code{\link[=period_included]{period_included()}}.

\code{period_included()} resolves the interval for a single individual's facts
\code{.i}. It is the plain-R reference path for testing and understanding, not
the performance path. An offset that is \code{NA} yields the empty interval.
}

\examples{
period_included(period(2010, 2020), .i = list())
